WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.580
Narrator: In the iconic bridge fight

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:03.480
from "Spider-Man: No Way Home,"

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.980
Doctor Octopus' tentacles were the work

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.730
of the visual effects team, but on set,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.418
the car and these exploding
water barrels were very real.

00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:14.640
Scott Edelstein: Even if we're
going to replace all that

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.110
and have digital versions of things,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.680
if you can shoot something,
it's always better.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.310
Narrator: That's visual effects supervisor

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.853
Scott Edelstein. His team collaborated

00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:25.162
with special effects supervisor Dan Sudick

00:00:25.162 --> 00:00:27.830
to find the right mix
of practical and digital

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.230
to create "No Way Home"'s
action-packed bridge fight,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.220
like when Doc Octopus first emerges

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.320
with his mechanical arms.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.170
To really sell the
strength of these CGI arms,

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.270
Dan devised way to crush
the cars practically

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.780
into what the crew called "taco cars."

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.930
When I saw the previs, I thought,
"Wow, wouldn't it be great

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.140
if we could just pull down the
center of the car very hard

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.440
and just let the car fold up by itself?"

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.630
Narrator: First, Dan
built a steel platform

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.100
with a hole in the middle.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.930
Then he placed the car on top of it

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.120
and attached two cables to
the bottom center of the car,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.593
pulling it through while it split in half.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.650
Shots like this one --

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.616
Hello, Peter.

00:01:05.616 --> 00:01:06.900
Narrator: Where Doc Ock was simply

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.110
suspended in the air,
were equally challenging.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.630
Unlike in 2004's "Spider-Man 2,"

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.580
Alfred Molina did not wear a pair

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.750
of puppeteered claws on set.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.600
While the actor now had more
flexibility to move around,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.083
Digital Domain would have to know

00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:23.083
how to place his arms in the shot,

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.420
especially when they
held him up like this.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.190
The best visual effects references

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.200
depended on how high his
body was above the ground,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.740
which varied throughout.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.230
Sometimes the crew could
use cables to lift him up,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.080
letting him move his real
legs around more freely,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.070
but it wasn't very comfortable.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.440
Other times he was
strapped to a tuning fork,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.570
where crew members could steer
and turn him from behind,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.600
as seen here, when he pulled himself up

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.980
from under the bridge.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.670
When the arms brought
him down to the ground,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.900
they used a moving platform
that could be lowered

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.390
and operated like a Technocrane.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.700
This got even trickier for the VFX team

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.250
as the sequence progressed
and the character interacted

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.150
more and more with his surroundings.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.620
Scott: Jon Watts, the
director, really wants to have

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.310
his movements make sense
and to have weight,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.885
so you don't want him to feel light

00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:09.760
or any of the things that
he's interacting with

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:10.640
to feel light.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.020
Narrator: This meant
establishing clear physics

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.100
about what he could and couldn't do.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.260
For example, he always had at least

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.190
two hands on the ground
supporting him for balance,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.650
even as he lifted two cars at once.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.010
The way he handled objects

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.240
also needed careful consideration.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.179
He steps forward and throws a car,

00:02:28.179 --> 00:02:29.522
he has to transfer that weight,

00:02:29.522 --> 00:02:32.572
and another arm has to
go land on the ground

00:02:32.572 --> 00:02:34.600
to hold him up as he's
throwing the car forward.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.730
Narrator: The practical team
also applied these rules

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.741
to props used in the fight, like here,

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:41.620
when Doc Ock throws a
gigantic pipe at Spider-Man

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.820
and instead crushes a car.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.420
Dan and lead visual effects
supervisor Kelly Port

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.740
wanted the pipe to fall
like a baseball bat,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.300
so it had to practically collapse

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.150
at an angle rather than flat.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.850
It basically is faster at the tip

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:54.960
and slower at the handle.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.530
Narrator: To achieve this unique effect,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.300
Dan used two cables to hold

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.380
the concrete and steel pipe straight.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.700
Each cable was attached
to a pneumatic cylinder,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.210
which released air pressure
at different speeds.

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:08.610
We could force the tip of
the pipe down into the car

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.630
faster than the front
of the pipe would fall,

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.970
and then we'd pull the front
of the pipe at a certain speed.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.910
Narrator: But that only took them so far.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.010
During an initial test, the pipe crushed

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.360
the top of the car, but not its sides,

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.830
so the sides were actually
weakened beforehand

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.660
by cutting out the doorframes.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.660
Then the crew hid cables inside the car,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.898
so when the pipe collapsed,

00:03:26.898 --> 00:03:29.960
the cables brought the sides
of the car down with it.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.500
Now, it would've been too
dangerous for Tom Holland

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.900
and his doubles to
actually dodge that pipe,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.010
so for this shot, the
action elements in the frame

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.470
were shot separately and
combined in postproduction.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.240
In one take, Tom rolled
over the hood of a car

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.860
to make it look like he
was dodging the pipe.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.045
Then the crew shot the pipe rig on its own

00:03:48.045 --> 00:03:49.987
while replicating the
speed and positioning

00:03:49.987 --> 00:03:52.310
of the camera as closely as possible.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.740
We track the camera within
all those environments,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.690
and we do a lot of reprojecting

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.638
so that we can bring it all
into one camera, basically.

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:01.300
Narrator: In the end, changes in the edit

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.060
meant Digital Domain had to
make this a fully CG shot,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.800
but many of the original
movements from the camera

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.120
and the actor are still there.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.640
We do try, even if we're
going to exaggerate it,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.990
to use the base of what he's
done and then embellish it.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.890
Narrator: Spider-Man also has to rescue

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.140
the assistant vice chancellor from her car

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.890
as it teeters over the edge of the bridge.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.940
The entire stunt was
broken up into three parts:

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.570
the car going over the bridge,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:30.520
the car crashing through the guardrail,

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.680
and the car dangling in the air.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.520
This required building a whole new set.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.580
While the main stretch of
highway was on the ground,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.658
this one was elevated 20 feet,

00:04:39.658 --> 00:04:42.040
so the car could hang
without hitting anything.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.350
First, the car was placed

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.530
on a small track to move it forward.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.160
Then it was guided by cables

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.382
for the moment it sputters out of control.

00:04:49.382 --> 00:04:50.517
Dan: We wanted it to

00:04:50.517 --> 00:04:52.675
look a little more natural when it got hit

00:04:52.675 --> 00:04:54.850
and let it kind of just
swing into the railing

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.610
as opposed to just follow this exact arc.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.610
Narrator: To make the car
crash through the guardrail,

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.430
Dan built a piece of the
rail out of beaded foam.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.420
He then painted it and plastered the edges

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.040
before pre-breaking it
into smaller pieces.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.580
Finally, he put it back together

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.490
so the car could smash through it.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.830
Dan: We built 20 or 25 feet of breakaway

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.113
because we figured the length of the car

00:05:15.113 --> 00:05:16.760
was 16, 17 feet long.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.560
Narrator: The car was
later placed on a gimbal

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.843
in front of a blue screen,

00:05:19.843 --> 00:05:22.001
so it would look like
it was really teetering

00:05:22.001 --> 00:05:24.550
at a 90-degree angle over the edge.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.260
The gimbal was safe enough
for actress Paula Newsome

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.170
to be inside the car,
so cameras could capture

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.690
her terrified facial expressions.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.380
Filming her upside down
was particularly helpful

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.950
for the VFX artists.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:38.910
You really get the dynamics with her hair

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.137
and the movement of her
clothes and all that.

00:05:41.137 --> 00:05:43.680
Narrator: And instead of
looking out at Spider-Man,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.230
she's actually looking at a tennis ball,

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.520
which was then easily
erased in postproduction.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.240
As Spider-Man tries to
pull her car to safety,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.690
Doc Ock throws another car at him,

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.840
which hits some barrels instead.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.750
According to Dan,

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.575
the director wanted it
to be raining water,

00:06:00.575 --> 00:06:04.210
so Dan had to manipulate
both the car and the barrels.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.150
This required putting a
20-foot nitrogen cannon

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:08.910
diagonally through the car.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.002
That cannon was attached

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.360
to a high-pressure accumulator
to launch it forward.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.110
Dan also filled the water barrels

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.716
with pyrotechnics linked to a timer.

00:06:17.716 --> 00:06:20.530
Dan: We knew the speed of the
car going into the barrels,

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.747
so we knew how many tenths
of a second it would take

00:06:22.747 --> 00:06:25.220
for the car to hit all the barrels.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.890
Narrator: Once the car
hit the first barrel,

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:28.535
each one blew up in order

00:06:28.535 --> 00:06:30.820
at the speed the car was coming at them.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.900
The practical stunt looked great,

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.370
but the trajectory was slightly off.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.860
So using the original shot as reference,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.060
Scott actually replaced the
car with a completely CG model.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.240
We needed the car to start higher,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.300
because Doc was further down the road

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.670
and he was up on his arms.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.110
The car needed to kind of roll

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.860
as it was going towards Spider-Man.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:53.700
Narrator: Many of these fighting shots

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.620
actually used digital doubles,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.790
which worked out, since
the nanotech-powered

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.350
Iron Spider Suit was created with CG.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.380
You've outdone yourself, Peter.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.000
Narrator: But because
Spider-Man removes his mask,

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.450
they couldn't just do a full body swap.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.680
Like the assistant vice
chancellor on the gimbal,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.230
they also needed to film
Tom hanging in the air.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.500
The way he moves his body,
tilts his neck, holds himself,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.387
is all reminiscent of somebody
actually hanging upside down.

00:07:19.387 --> 00:07:21.740
Narrator: But the constant
movement of the action

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.690
made it hard to accurately
place the iconic costume.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.620
So Tom wore what's
known as a fractal suit.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.520
The patterns on the suit gave animators

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.810
the easiest way to map the digital body

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:34.470
onto the actor's body.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.550
If his chest turns or moves
or his arms are moving,

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:39.630
you can see those patterns moving easier

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.870
than if he was just in a plain suit.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.570
Narrator: For the
tentacles, Doc Ock's jacket

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.000
had holes in the back of it.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.120
These red tracking marks allowed VFX

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.780
to accurately place the arms,

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.380
despite the constant
moving camera and action.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:52.980
You can locate where the arm is

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.897
and really stick it to that little dot,

00:07:54.897 --> 00:07:56.390
because if it's swimming around,

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.720
then it looks like it's
swimming around on his back.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.650
Narrator: After pulling the
assistant vice chancellor's

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.350
car up, Spidey uses his web blasters

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.470
to pull the car door off.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.090
The web was a fully CG creation,

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.910
but on set, the special effects team

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:11.400
needed to create enough force

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.490
to take the door off by itself.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.560
That first meant replacing its hinge pins

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:17.370
with ones made out of balsa wood.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.862
The door was then attached
to a cable from the outside

00:08:19.862 --> 00:08:22.190
that was powered by a pneumatic piston.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:24.470
The accumulator lets the
air rush into the piston,

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.254
piston closes, pulls a
cable, the door comes off.

00:08:27.254 --> 00:08:29.010
Narrator: Pre-destroying cars off-set

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.700
was also useful for the moment

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.070
when the Green Goblin's
pumpkin bomb explodes.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:35.920
The cars were actually taken apart

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.510
and then put back together
before being brought to set,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.970
allowing for these dramatic results.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.890
And it was up to Scott and his team

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:45.740
to enhance all these
crashes and explosions

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:47.290
while populating the shot

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.570
and digitally extending the bridge.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.590
According to Scott, Digital Domain created

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.740
250 static cars parked on the bridge,

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.990
plus 1,100 digital cars

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:59.130
driving around the city in the distance.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.020
Those cars are all variations

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:03.560
of just a handful of digital car models.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.050
Meanwhile, the cars that
were closest to the camera

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.330
needed to be digitally scanned.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.931
They do a basic texture
shoot of photography

00:09:10.931 --> 00:09:13.230
for what the car looks
like, what the texture is.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.830
Narrator: When adding
these digital cars in,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.505
each car is then customized

00:09:16.505 --> 00:09:19.463
based on the action on-screen
to ensure continuity,

00:09:19.463 --> 00:09:21.663
so the team had to keep
track of everything

00:09:21.663 --> 00:09:23.900
from dirt stains to the exact position

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.610
where a car was originally placed.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.130
Scott: Well, there's a
whole lot that goes into

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.120
just tracking where everything lives.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.920
Narrator: So, why even bother shooting

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.690
time-consuming and
complicated effects for real

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.280
if so many of them are replaced digitally?

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:40.370
It's all invaluable reference
for some very unique ideas,

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:43.570
like how glass looks
after a car gets crushed.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.240
Scott: The way that the metal bends,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.790
it's not always super sharp.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.880
A lot of times it's kind of softer curves.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.260
The side windows sometimes

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:51.470
when it's getting crushed like that

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.748
literally explode into a bajillion

00:09:53.748 --> 00:09:55.177
little tiny safety pieces.

00:09:55.177 --> 00:09:56.860
Narrator: And you never
know what real elements

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.380
can make their way into the final shot.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.210
We kept the water barrels exploding,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.090
so in that particular shot,
all that water is real.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.580
And we had to remove the practical car

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:10.050
from inside of all that
water and add our CG car.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:12.960
Tom Holland is extraordinarily athletic

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.120
and did a lot of his own stunts.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.860
Whether we up using the
entire performance or not,

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.303
it's really good reference for us.

00:10:19.303 --> 00:10:22.440
Or we do use it, and it's
great to see his physicality

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.160
and how his body moves
and what he's doing,

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.510
because a lot of his
performance is coming from that.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:27.543
Now you die.

00:10:30.548 --> 00:10:32.580
[Peter groaning]

